  palindromic beta, Euclidean: dU/dg.d = -5.21488611  vs (V1-T)/2 Tr = -5.21488611; dU/df.d = -5.21488611 vs T/2 Tr = -5.21488611; |Im| < 0e+00
  generic beta, Lorentzian: dU/dg.d = +3.14190528  vs (V1-T)/2 Tr = +3.14190528; dU/df.d = +1.53390449 vs T/2 Tr = +1.53390449; |Im| < 0e+00
  palindromic beta, Lorentzian: dU/dg.d = +10.56804747  vs (V1-T)/2 Tr = +10.56804746; dU/df.d = +10.56804746 vs T/2 Tr = +10.56804746; |Im| < 0e+00
  U(g,f) - U(f,g): palindromic 0.0e+00 | generic 4.7e+01
  palindromic sample: T_pal = -4.528712 -> aligned Lambda = m^4 T/M^2, not zero unless tuned

========================================================================
E. Generic bimetric with the ratio c free: how many tunings for a flat proportional vacuum?
========================================================================
resultant(P_g, P_f; c) is a single polynomial condition on the betas; nonzero: True | total degree 6
random beta1..4 = [ 0.1417  2.8084 -0.6171 -0.1909], positive root c* = 3.388958, tuned beta0 = -74.184317
  P_g(c*) = -3.6e-15, P_f(c*) = 2.2e-14, V(c*) = 7.8e-14
  after rescaling f by 1/c*^2: beta' = [-74.1843   0.4802  32.2543 -24.0194 -25.1849]; L_g = -3.6e-15, L_f = 7.8e-14 (flat, aligned); palindromic? False
  R(s^2 f)/R(f) = s**(-2) ; sqrt|det(s^2 f)|/sqrt|det f| = s**4
